Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (Mild Transient)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Myalgia, mild fever, positive anti-histone antibodies, resolves upon drug cessation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-013
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of fever and severe muscle aching requiring exclusion of systemic sepsis or acute influenza.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute systemic symptoms (fever, myalgia) in a patient taking chronic medications. Immediate clinical review was necessary to distinguish a severe infectious process or systemic bacteremia from an autoimmune drug reaction, determining the immediate need for hospital admission and intravenous antibiotics.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My whole body is aching terribly, I'm burning up, and I feel too weak to even get out of a chair.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports lupus-like symptoms following medication use. The timeline involves feeling completely well until this afternoon when profound myalgia and malaise rapidly developed, progressing to subjective fever and mild arthralgia over 4 hours. Patient was prescribed hydralazine for hypertension 8 weeks ago. Denies cough, dysuria, focal pain, or recent travel. The rapid onset of systemic symptoms prompted fears of severe acute infection.
Functional Status: Markedly reduced due to severe muscle pain, mobilizing with extreme difficulty.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, high falls risk secondary to acute weakness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, but appearing fatigued and moving deliberately to avoid muscle pain.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals diffuse, symmetrical myalgia with deep palpation over major muscle groups. Joint exam shows mild tenderness but no frank synovitis, effusions, or erythema. Myalgia, mild fever, positive anti-histone antibodies (presumed/pending), resolves upon drug cessation (plan). Respiratory, cardiovascular, and abdominal examinations are entirely unremarkable, showing no localized source of infection. No rash identified.
Environmental Observations: Safe home environment, adequate heating, clear walkways.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has been fully compliant with hydralazine 50mg TID.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (Mild Transient) secondary to Hydralazine - Status: Acute Onset
Differentials:

Influenza/Viral Myositis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of upper respiratory symptoms, coryza, cough, or sore throat.

Systemic Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal hemodynamics (blood pressure maintained, non-tachycardic relative to temperature), and absence of any focal infectious source.

Acute Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient's younger age profile and the very abrupt onset strictly correlating with the known timeline for DILE.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to acute functional decline while living alone)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute febrile myalgia, heavily mimicking a systemic viral illness or early sepsis. Triage correctly mandated urgent evaluation to exclude a life-threatening infectious etiology. Careful physical examination localized no infectious source and maintained normal hemodynamics. Integrating the medication history confirmed the high probability of a transient drug-induced autoimmune reaction. This assessment was clinically vital to halt the offending medication immediately and safely avert an inappropriate emergency department presentation for suspected sepsis, satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Directed immediate cessation of hydralazine. Administered oral NSAIDs for acute myalgia and fever control. Blood pressure stabilized.
Positive Behavioral Change: Take the prescribed anti-inflammatory medication strictly with a full glass of water and a small snack. By adhering to this routine, you will protect your stomach lining while experiencing rapid relief from the severe muscle pain, allowing you to move freely again within hours.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24-48 hours for blood pressure medication switch and ANA/anti-histone antibody testing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department if you develop severe chest pain, shortness of breath, a rapidly spreading rash, or if your fever spikes above 39.0 degrees Celsius.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending specific antibody testing; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The symptoms are a direct reaction to the blood pressure medication and will completely resolve within days to weeks of stopping the drug.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you feel dizzy or lightheaded upon standing, or if your pain becomes unbearable despite taking the anti-inflammatory medication.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is temporarily reacting to the hydralazine. Stopping the drug stops the reaction. The anti-inflammatory will manage the pain in the meantime.
Actionable Steps: Do not take any more hydralazine. Rest completely tomorrow. You must see your regular doctor promptly to get a safe replacement for your blood pressure medication.

